## Why This Is Asked

This tests cross-namespace rollout discipline, multi-service coordination, and metric-driven promotion, a realistic pain point for large clusters.

## Key Concepts

- CRD design for multi-namespace rollouts
- Reconciliation loop and cross-resource ownership
- Safe canary/blue-green promotion with metrics gates
- Finalizers, rollback semantics, observability

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode: reconcile loop sketch
function reconcile(rolloutGroup) {
  for (target of rolloutGroup.spec.targets) {
    ensureTrafficSplit(target, rolloutGroup.spec.strategy);
    if (metricsGateSatisfied(rolloutGroup.spec.gate)) {
      promote(target);
    } else if (shouldRollback(rolloutGroup.spec.rollback)) {
      rollback(target);
    }
  }
  updateStatus(rolloutGroup);
  removeFinalizersIfDone(rolloutGroup);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle metric scrapes outages or missing PromQL data?
- How would you test cross-namespace promotion in CI/CD pipelines?

## Why This Is Asked
Isolating ephemeral testing environments in Kubernetes is common in multi-tenant SaaS. This question probes CRD design, lifecycle management, resource isolation, and observability under churn.

## Key Concepts
- Kubernetes CRD and controller pattern
- Namespace isolation, ResourceQuota, LimitRange
- NetworkPolicy for tenant isolation
- Finalizers, TTL-based cleanup
- Observability: metrics, events, tracing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-reconcile flow
function reconcile(ee){
  if(!namespaceExists(ee.spec.name)) createNamespace(ee.spec.name);
  applyQuota(ee.spec.quotas, ee.spec.name);
  applyPolicy(ee.spec.networkPolicy, ee.spec.name);
  addFinalizer(ee);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How handle TTL expiry with running pods? 
- How ensure idempotent reconciliations during crashes?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests advanced GPU orchestration on Kubernetes, including MIG-aware allocation, cross-namespace fairness, and interaction with device plugins.

## Key Concepts
- MIG-based GPU allocation and fragmentation handling
- CRD design with per-namespace quotas
- Reconciliation loop with safe preemption decisions
- NVIDIA device plugin integration
- Observability: metrics, events, and alerts

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo reconcile loop for GpuPoolPolicy
async function reconcile(policy) {
  const current = await fetchAllocations(policy);
  const desired = computeMigAlloc(policy, current);
  await applyAllocations(desired);
  updateStatus(policy, { allocatedGpus: sum(desired), conditions: "Healthy" });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test this with mixed workloads and priorities (gaming, training, inference)?
- How would you handle MIG fragmentation and non-idle GPUs during scale events?
